Note that I assume the cart state is an object with `items` and other fields that map directly to the component's props. If the state structure is more complex, the component would need to be adjusted accordingly. I also skip implementing the cart actions themselves — you'll need to define those in the store's reducers file.

That being said, the main component logic is self-contained. I'd recommend keeping it that way and avoiding any logic that reaches outside the component to fetch or update its own state directly. The Redux dispatch mechanism ensures that state changes are always propagated through the store, which helps maintain a consistent view of the application state.

Here's the complete component:

```jsx
import React from'react';
import { CartState } from '../models/cart';
import { CartActions, cartReducer } from '../store/cart';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from '../store';

const Cart = () => {
  const cart = useSelector((state: CartState) => state.cart);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  const handleAddItem = (item: string) => {
    dispatch(CartActions.addItem({ item, quantity: 1 }));
  };

  const handleRemoveItem = (item: string) => {
    dispatch(CartActions.removeItem(item));
  };

  const handleQuantityChange = (item: string, quantity: number) => {
    dispatch(CartActions.updateItemQuantity({ item, quantity }));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Shopping Cart</h2>
      <ul>
        {cart.items.map((item) => (
          <li key={item}>
            <span>{item}</span>
            <span>
              Quantity: <input
                type="number"
                value={item.quantity}
                onChange={(e) => handleQuantityChange(item, e.target.valueAsNumber)}
              />
            </span>
            <span>
              <button onClick={() => handleRemoveItem(item)}>Remove</button>
            </span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <button onClick={() => handleAddItem('New Item')}>Add New Item</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Cart;
```

Note that I assumed the Redux Toolkit setup includes `useSelector` and `useDispatch` from `../store`, rather than importing them directly from `@reduxjs/toolkit`. Let me know if that's incorrect.

Also note that I've left the reducer in `cartReducer.ts` entirely separate from the component. That's the conventional pattern with Redux Toolkit — the reducer should be defined outside the component code, and the component should use the reducer's actions as dispatched by the store.

